SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine Financial Institutions Superintendent w
 arns consumers of potential financial scams during the holiday season
DESCRIPTION:Maine Financial Institutions Superintendent warns consumers of
  potential financial scams during the holiday season\nDec 1\, 2021\nGARDIN
 ER – As the holiday season approaches\, Maine’s Bureau of Financial
  Institutions Superintendent Lloyd LaFountain is reminding consumers to b
 e on the lookout for potential scams targeting consumers’ financial inf
 ormation\, including their debit and credit cards:\n“The holidays are an
  active time of year for scammers seeking to target unsuspecting consumers
  and illegally gain access to their finances. Consumers should remain vigi
 lant throughout the season’s festivities to ensure their money is well 
 protected and secure through the holiday season.”\nWhile scams can targe
 t consumers at any time\, Superintendent LaFountain notes that scammer act
 ivity may increase during the holiday season\, when peoples’ busy sched
 ules and holiday shopping leaves them with less time to watch their finan
 ces. Consumers should continue to monitor their bank accounts and report a
 ny unauthorized charges to their financial institutions. Also\, holiday sh
 oppers should beware of gift card scams\, and know that a reputable financ
 ial institution\, government agency\, or utility company will never ask yo
 u to pay an outstanding bill or charge using a gift card.\n“Best practic
 es for using debit and credit cards throughout the year should be followed
  during the holidays as well. Consumers are reminded to never provide thei
 r PIN numbers\, online banking passwords\, and personal information to ano
 ther person or company that appears untrustworthy. Credit unions and banks
  will never text\, call\, or email a consumer requesting this information.
  Do not reply to unsolicited emails. A good rule of thumb is to contact yo
 ur bank or credit union directly if you receive a suspicious message.\nMan
 y consumers see the holiday season as the perfect time to give to their fa
 vorite charities. Superintendent LaFountain urges consumers to be wary of 
 fake charities targeting their goodwill. Consumers lookin\nhttps://www.mai
 ne.gov/pfr/financialinstitutions/news-notices/2026-09-17-maine-financial-i
 nstitutions-superintendent-warns-consumers-potential
URL:https://www.maine.gov/pfr/financialinstitutions/news-notices/2026-09-1
 7-maine-financial-institutions-superintendent-warns-consumers-potential
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f8fd9d7539cc435f@role-securities-financial.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260917T212009Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20180402
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine’s Bureau of Financial Institutions wa
 rns public about online banking update scams
DESCRIPTION:Maine’s Bureau of Financial Institutions warns public about 
 online banking update scams\nApr 2\, 2018\nGARDINER – Maine’s Bureau 
 of Financial Institutions at the Department of Professional and Financial
  Regulation warns of a scam involving updates to financial institutions’
  online banking platforms. The messages\, typically sent to a consumer’
 s email address\, appear to come from a consumer’s financial institut
 ion\, claiming that the bank or credit union is updating its online bankin
 g platform and that the consumer needs to provide verification of account 
 information. The scammers request that the consumer click through links in
  the email\, which direct the user to a site asking for sensitive account 
 information. The messages threaten immediate suspension of the consumer
 ’s access to online banking services unless the account information is 
 provided.\nThe Bureau of Financial Institutions reminds consumers to look 
 out for this scam and not to divulge bank or credit union account numbers 
 or other personal information by email\, text\, or phone. The customers of
  several banks and credit unions received emails related to this scam\, an
 d anyone with an email account is a potential target. “Banks and credit
  unions will not email\, text\, or call customers asking them to divulge 
 account numbers\, pins\, or social security numbers\,” Superintendent 
 Lloyd P. LaFountain III said.\nLaFountain emphasized that customers receiv
 ing unexpected e-mails regarding online banking updates should call their 
 bank or credit union directly and talk to an employee. Financial instituti
 on employees will be able to inform the consumer if the bank or credit uni
 on is currently updating its online banking system. The Superintendent als
 o noted that customers should always be vigilant to protect their personal
  information and monitor account statements.\nIf a consumer suspects he or
  she has received a scam email\, the consumer should:\nNot click through a
 ny links in the email. Clicking the link itself may expose the con\nhttps
 ://www.maine.gov/pfr/financialinstitutions/news-notices/2026-09-17-maines-
 bureau-financial-institutions-warns-public-about-online-banking
URL:https://www.maine.gov/pfr/financialinstitutions/news-notices/2026-09-1
 7-maines-bureau-financial-institutions-warns-public-about-online-banking
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
